schedule(static) aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:64)\\n            for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n                px[i] += vx[i] * DT;\\n                py[i] += vy[i] * DT;\\n                pz[i] += vz[i] * DT;\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    float *out = (float *)malloc(count * sizeof(float));\\n    if (!out) { perror(\\\"malloc\\\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n        out[6*i]   = px[i];\\n        out[6*i+1] = py[i];\\n        out[6*i+2] = pz[i];\\n        out[6*i+3] = vx[i];\\n        out[6*i+4] = vy[i];\\n        out[6*i+5] = vz[i];\\n    }\\n    if (fwrite(out, count * sizeof(float), 1, stdout) != 1) {\\n        fprintf(stderr, \\\"\\\\nError writing to output\\\\n\\\");\\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n    }\\n\\n    free(px); free(py); free(pz); free(vx); free(vy); free(vz); free(out);\\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\\n}\\n\"\n\nWe need to escape double quotes inside strings: e.g., \"malloc\" becomes \\\"malloc\\\", and \"\\nError reading...\" becomes \\\"\\\\nError reading...\\\"? Actually the string contains backslash-n as two characters in C source; when we put it in JSON, we need to represent that as \\\\n (backslash followed by n) because JSON interprets \\n as newline. So we must escape backslashes: each backslash becomes double backslash. In our content above, there are strings like \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\" which contain literal backslash-n characters in C source. To represent that in JSON string, we need to write \"\\\\nError reading nbodie value\\\\n\". Similarly for other strings.\n\nAlso the comment lines have no special chars except maybe //; fine.\n\nWe'll construct the content with proper escaping: replace each \\ with \\\\ and each \" with \\\" but careful not to double-escape already escaped? We'll do it manually.\n\nLet's write the JSON string carefully. I'll produce a single line with all escapes.\n\nI'll copy the code above and escape:\n\n- Replace every backslash (\\) with two ba